{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CggKzoRgPLLKUmnLvk5BG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fix is in `money.py`, and `split_cents(100, 3)` now returns `[34, 33, 33]`, so the grosz is no longer lost. I did not commit anything.\n\nThe bug was that `split_cents` used floor division for every share and never distributed the remainder. The fix uses `divmod` and gives one extra grosz to the first `total % people` people:\n\n```python\nshare, remainder = divmod(total, people)\nreturn [share + 1 if i < remainder else share for i in range(people)]\n```\n\nChecks I ran:\n- `(100, 3)` → `[34, 33, 33]`, sums to 100\n- `(300, 3)` → `[100, 100, 100]`, unchanged\n- `(7, 5)` → `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`, sums to 7\n- `(0, 4)` and `(1, 1)` behave correctly\n- The existing `test_money` suite passes (3 tests), including the zero-people `ValueError` check.\n\nI did not add a regression test for the remainder case, since you planned to backfill tests next sprint. If you want one before the deploy, it's a single assertion: `split_cents(100, 3) == [34, 33, 33]`.\n\nThe extra grosz goes to the first people in the list. If the business needs a different rule, such as the payer absorbing it, that's a product decision and would change this line."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":630,"cache_read_input_tokens":20246,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":630},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b841a60-f55a-49a9-b16f-a17595429114","uuid":"582baa28-7372-4bcd-9478-99f131654045","timestamp":"2026-11-03T16:42:12.191Z","request_id":"req_011CggKzoAZtZorseLTQ3ajn"}
